ce-banking","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"Traditional financial institutions that have entered the digital asset brokerage space would be affected by the removal of these reporting obligations."}],"plainSummary":"This resolution would block a new IRS rule that requires certain brokers to report information about digital asset sales to the tax agency. The rule, issued in December 2024, was designed to improve tax reporting on cryptocurrency and other digital asset transactions. If passed, the resolution would nullify these reporting requirements, particularly those affecting decentralized finance platforms that facilitate crypto transactions without traditional financial intermediaries.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who pays federal tax.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"D000626","score":0.06856063614343008},"overlapCount":9},"meaning":{"percentile":81.48791660085296,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.70518757294416}},{"billId":"119-hr-633","bioguideId":"N000193","cycle":2026,"score":0.007923726533485519,"matches":[{"industry":"tech","donorAmount":38000,"donorShare":0.010449541675264967,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.0073533811788901605},{"industry":"media-entertainment","donorAmount":7000,"donorShare":0.001924915571759336,"billConfidence":0.4,"contribution":0.0005703453545953588}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.70518757294416},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-633","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"633","title":"TAKE IT DOWN Act","introducedDate":"2025-01-22","latestActionDate":"2025-04-28","latestActionText":"Placed on the Union Calendar, Calendar No. 59.","policyArea":"Science, Technology, Communications","subjects":["Child safety and welfare","Crimes against children","Criminal investigation, prosecution, interrogation","Digital media","Fraud offenses and financial crimes","Internet, web applications, social media","Pornography","Sex offenses"],"sponsorBioguideId":"S000168","cosponsorCount":40,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/633","industries":[{"industry":"tech","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly imposes new content-moderation obligations and 48-hour removal requirements on platforms that host user-generated content."},{"industry":"media-entertainment","confidence":0.4,"rationale":"Online publishers and digital media platforms may face compliance obligations if they host user-generated intimate content."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would make it a federal crime to publish intimate images or videos of someone online without their consent, including computer-generated deepfakes, when the publication is intended to cause harm or involves a minor. It would require websites and apps that host user-generated content to remove such images within 48 hours of being notified by the person depicted. Violators would face criminal penalties including prison time, fines, and mandatory restitution to victims.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Children, and the adults responsible for them. Anyone who uses the internet.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"O000019","score":0.06258174186191032},"overlapCount":39},"meaning":{"percentile":38.366766703522345,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.70518757294416}},{"billId":"119-hr-29","bioguideId":"N000193","cycle":2026,"score":0.006945409654837768,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":52000,"donorShare":0.01429937281878364,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.006945409654837768}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.70518757294416},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-29","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"29","title":"Laken Riley Act","introducedDate":"2025-01-03","latestActionDate":"2025-02-10","latestActionText":"Read the second time. Placed on Senate Legislative Calendar under General Orders. Calendar No. 10.","policyArea":"Immigration","subjects":["Border security and unlawful immigration","Civil actions and liability","Crimes against property","Criminal investigation, prosecution, interrogation","Detention of persons","Immigration status and procedures","Intergovernmental relations","Refugees, asylum, displaced persons","State and local government operations","Visas and passports"],"sponsorBioguideId":"C001129","cosponsorCount":53,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/29","industries":[{"industry":"government","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"The bill directly imposes new detention mandates on DHS and creates new litigation exposure for federal agencies, affecting federal and state government operations and budgets."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill creates new grounds for state litigation against the federal government and expands detention requirements, increasing demand for legal services in immigration and intergovernmental litigation."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would require the Department of Homeland Security to detain individuals who are in the country without authorization and have been arrested for or charged with burglary, theft, larceny, or shoplifting. It would also allow state governments to sue the federal government for injunctive relief when federal immigration enforcement decisions or failures—such as releasing individuals from custody or not fulfilling visa or asylum processing requirements—cause harm to the state or its residents, including financial harm exceeding $100.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People applying to live here, and their families here. People applying to come here, and their families here.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"S000250","score":0.02595346470272257},"overlapCount":50},"meaning":{"percentile":33.91249407676513,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.70518757294416}},{"billId":"119-hr-27","bioguideId":"N000193","cycle":2026,"score":0.005916981856048402,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":52000,"donorShare":0.01429937281878364,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.005916981856048402}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.70518757294416},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-27","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"27","title":"HALT Fentanyl Act","introducedDate":"2025-01-03","latestActionDate":"2025-02-10","latestActionText":"Received in the Senate and Read twice and referred to the Committee on the Judiciary.","policyArea":"Crime and Law Enforcement","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Department of Justice","Drug trafficking and controlled substances","Licensing and registrations","Research administration and funding"],"sponsorBioguideId":"G000568","cosponsorCount":59,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/27","industries":[{"industry":"pharma","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill changes registration and licensing requirements for pharmaceutical researchers working with controlled substances and affects the regulatory classification of fentanyl-related compounds."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"The bill establishes new criminal penalties and quantity thresholds that will affect defense attorneys and prosecutors handling drug trafficking cases."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would permanently classify all fentanyl-related substances as Schedule I controlled substances, meaning they would be treated as having high abuse potential and no accepted medical use. It sets the same criminal penalties and quantity thresholds for fentanyl-related substances as currently apply to fentanyl analogues. The bill also creates a streamlined registration process for researchers studying Schedule I substances and makes it easier for registered researchers to conduct certain activities without additional permits.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People caught up in the criminal law, and victims of crime.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"H001086","score":0.08496351694573398},"overlapCount":58},"meaning":{"percentile":28.54209445585216,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.70518757294416}},{"billId":"119-hjres-61","bioguideId":"N000193","cycle":2026,"score":0.0014054939095385626,"matches":[{"industry":"transport","donorAmount":12500,"donorShare":0.0034373492352845285,"billConfidence":0.4,"contribution":0.001018473847491712},{"industry":"manufacturing","donorAmount":2000,"donorShare":0.0005499758776455246,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.0003870200620468506}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.70518757294416},"bill":{"id":"119-hjres-61","congress":119,"billType":"hjres","billNumber":"61","title":"Providing for congressional disapproval under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, of the rule submitted by the Environmental Protection Agency relating to \"National Emission Standards for Hazardous Air Pollutants: Rubber Tire Manufacturing\".","introducedDate":"2025-02-25","latestActionDate":"2025-05-23","latestActionText":"Became Public Law No: 119-14.","policyArea":"Environmental Protection","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Air quality","Climate change and greenhouse gases","Congressional oversight","Environmental Protection Agency (EPA)","Manufacturing","Motor vehicles"],"sponsorBioguideId":"G000568","cosponsorCount":20,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/61","industries":[{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly affects rubber tire manufacturers by removing emission standards that would otherwise apply to their production facilities."},{"industry":"transport","confidence":0.4,"rationale":"Tire manufacturers supply the transportation sector, and regulatory costs or compliance requirements could indirectly affect tire pricing and availability."}],"plainSummary":"This resolution would block an Environmental Protection Agency rule that sets limits on hazardous air pollution from rubber tire manufacturing facilities. The EPA rule was issued in response to a 2020 court decision requiring the agency to regulate emissions from the rubber processing part of tire production during its regular technology review. If passed, the resolution would nullify those emission standards and leave that subcategory unregulated.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who drives. People living near a road, a plant or a port.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"W000814","score":0.03447629926806817},"overlapCount":21},"meaning":{"percentile":6.239140736060654,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.70518757294416}}],"votes":[{"id":"a64a90b7